<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick III of Wied</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1618">1618</a>–<a href="/years/1698">1698</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich III of Wied</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick III of Wied was a German count who founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1653, near a village ruined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War. To attract settlers he promised freedom of religion and trade privileges, and Neuwied became a refuge for religious minorities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Brethren</a>, and the residence of his descendants.</p></div>
